Process management in Linux involves monitoring, prioritizing, terminating, and adjusting the resources allocated to running programs. Every running program is treated as a process and assigned a unique Process ID (PID).


1. Monitoring and Viewing Processes

These commands help you see what processes are running, who owns them, and how many system resources (CPU, RAM) they are consuming.
• ps (Process Status): Provides a static snapshot of current processes.
	• ps : Shows processes running in the current terminal session.
	• ps aux : Displays all running processes across the entire system for all users.
	• ps -ef : Displays all processes with full detail, including parent-child relationships (PPID).
• top: Provides a real-time, dynamic view of system activity, sorted automatically by CPU usage.
	• Shortcuts inside top: Press q to quit, M to sort by memory, and P to sort by CPU.
• htop: An advanced, interactive, and user-friendly text-mode process viewer. It features color-coded bars for CPU/RAM usage and supports mouse clicks. (May require installation: sudo apt install htop or sudo dnf install htop).



2. Terminating and Controlling Processes

If a process becomes unresponsive or needs to be stopped, Linux uses signals to communicate with it.
• kill <PID>: Sends a signal to a process using its PID. By default, it sends SIGTERM (15), which asks the program to exit gracefully.
• kill -9 <PID>: Sends SIGKILL (9), which forces the process to terminate immediately without saving data or cleaning up.
• pkill <pattern>: Terminates processes based on their name or a pattern match instead of a PID (e.g., pkill firefox).
• killall <name>: Kills all instances of a process that share the exact specified name.


3. Managing Priorities (Niceness)

Linux prioritizes processes using a Nice value, which ranges from -20 (highest priority, least nice) to 19 (lowest priority, most nice). The default nice value is 0.
• nice: Starts a new process with a customized priority level.
	• Example: nice -n 10 official-script.sh (runs a script with lower priority, giving up CPU time to other tasks).
• renice: Modifies the priority of an already running process using its PID.
	• Example: renice -n -5 -p 1234 (increases the priority of PID 1234; requires root/sudo privileges)



4. Job Control (Foreground & Background)

When running commands via a terminal interface, you can move tasks between the foreground (blocking the terminal) and the background (running silently).
• & (Ampersand): Appending this to the end of any command runs it directly in the background.
	• Example: python3 script.py &
• Ctrl + Z: Suspends (pauses) the currently running foreground process and returns you to the prompt.
• jobs: Displays a list of all background or suspended jobs in the current shell session.
• bg %<job_id>: Resumes a suspended process, letting it run in the background.
• fg %<job_id>: Brings a background or suspended process back into the foreground.
• nohup: Runs a command that is immune to hangups (SIGHUP), meaning it will keep running even if you close the terminal or log out.